        "snapshot_id": 173,
        "final_answer": "## Investigation Result\n\n**No — execution does not reach `retry_delay` when the token is invalid.** The failure ordering is:\n\n1. **`submit_job` entry point** (`workflow.py`, lines 5–10) [E2_1] begins by calling `verify_token(token)` as its very first executable statement.\n2. **`verify_token`** (`auth.py`, lines 1–3) checks whether the token equals `\"trusted\"`; if it does not, it immediately raises a `PermissionError(\"Invalid token\")` [E4_1].\n3. Because that exception propagates uncaught, control never reaches the second line of `submit_job`, which is `delay = retry_delay(attempt, transient)` [E2_1].\n\nIn other words, the token validation guard sits *before* any call to `retry_delay`. An invalid token causes an early exit via exception long before the policy module (`policy.py`) or its `retry_delay` function [E6_1] is ever invoked.",
