      "grounding_review": "Codex source review, 2026-10-14: the answer still claims all permanent jobs are unconditionally rejected regardless of attempt, then contradicts that with the earlier negative-attempt exception. Token preconditions are omitted. A correct summary table does not erase the unsupported universal claim.",
      "review_hash": "fb24eb0692667589e558d29b2465ebc82d946cf6ba296dc16e5cec29281cb69a",
      "result": {
        "repository_id": 113,
        "snapshot_id": 173,
        "final_answer": "## Investigation Result\n\nThe behavior is entirely explained by the interaction between `submit_job` in **workflow.py** [E2_1] and `retry_delay` in **policy.py** [E4_1].\n\n### Why permanent jobs are rejected\n\nIn `policy.py`, `retry_delay` returns `None` for any non-transient job (`if not transient: return None`) [E4_1]. Then in `workflow.py`, `submit_job` checks `if delay is None` and returns `{\"status\": \"rejected\"}` [E2_1]. So **all permanent (non-transient) jobs are unconditionally rejected**, regardless of attempt number.\n\n### Why a transient job at attempt 10 gets scheduled with delay 300\n\nFor a transient job, `retry_delay` computes `min(2 ** attempt, 300)` [E4_1]:\n- At attempt 10: `2 ** 10 = 1024`, so `min(1024, 300) = 300`.\n\nThus the job is scheduled with `\"delay\": 300` [E2_1].\n\n### Negative-attempt boundary\n\nThe negative-attempt guard lives in `policy.py`: if `attempt < 0`, `retry_delay` raises `ValueError(\"Attempt must be nonnegative\")` before reaching the transient check [E4_1]. This means **negative attempts never reach the rejection logic** — they crash with an exception instead.\n\n### Summary\n\n| Scenario | Outcome | Reason |\n|---|---|---|\n| Permanent job (any attempt ≥ 0) | Rejected | `transient=False` → `retry_delay` returns `None` → `submit_job` rejects |\n| Transient job, attempt 10 | Scheduled, delay=300 | `transient=True` → `min(2^10, 300) = 300` |\n| Any job, attempt < 0 | Raises `ValueError` | Guard clause in `retry_delay` [E4_1] |",
