        "final_answer": "## Investigation\n\n### The Three Files\n\n1. **README.md** (E2_1) — The public retry guide documentation.\n2. **workflow.py** (E3_1) — The `submit_job` entry point that calls `retry_delay`.\n3. **policy.py** (E4_1) — The actual retry policy implementation.\n\n### How the Mismatch Arises\n\n| Layer | What it says / does |\n|---|---|\n| **README.md** (E5_1) | *\"The public retry guide claims transient jobs wait at most **60 seconds**.\"* |\n| **policy.py** (E6_1, E7_1) | `return min(2 ** attempt, 300)` — the cap is **300 seconds**, not 60. |\n| **workflow.py** (E3_1) | `submit_job` delegates to `retry_delay(attempt, transient)` and returns whatever delay it produces. |\n\nFor a transient job at **attempt 10**:\n\n- `2 ** 10 = 1024`\n- `min(1024, 300) = 300`\n\nSo `submit_job` returns `\"delay\": 300`, which contradicts the documented maximum of 60 seconds.\n\nThe root cause is straightforward: the policy file caps the exponential backoff at **300** seconds (`min(2 ** attempt, 300)`), while the public-facing README states the maximum should be **60** seconds. There is no additional guard in `workflow.py` or elsewhere that enforces the 60-second limit.\n\n### Proposed Resolution (without modifying code)\n\nSince this is a read-only investigation with no code changes permitted, the resolution must be external to the source files:\n\n1. **Update the documentation** — Change README.md to accurately reflect the implemented behavior (a 300-second cap), so the public retry guide matches reality. This is the safer immediate fix because changing the code would alter user-facing retry timing.\n2. **Or, align the policy with the promise** — If the 60-second cap is the intended product requirement, the `policy.py` expression should become `min(2 ** attempt, 60)`. However, since we cannot modify code, this must be proposed as a future change request (e.g., a ticket or PR) rather than executed here.\n\n**Recommendation:** Given that a 300-second cap already exists in production code and may have been deliberately chosen, the lowest-risk path is to **update the documentation first** to match the current behavior. Once verified, a separate change can introduce the 60-second cap if business requirements demand it.",
